    "innovation": "This review paper synthesizes the evidence for elevation-dependent warming, details the key physical mechanisms responsible, and outlines a strategy to address knowledge gaps through improved observations, remote sensing, and modeling.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "European Alps",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Regional variability in EDW could also be influenced by interannual to decadal variability in large-scale circulation; such as the El Ni\u00f1o/Southern Oscillation (ENSO), North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO), Pacific Decadal Oscillation (PDO), and so on. For instance, it has been found that during positive NAO winters in the late 1980s and early 1990s, there was enhanced warming at high elevations in the European Alps, associated with warm wet winters23. This pattern was reversed during years with a negative NAO index. In the tropical Andes, increasing freezing level heights between 1958 and 1990 were related to El Ni\u00f1o conditions (higher sea surface temperatures in the eastern tropical Pacific) and attendant changes in clouds and atmospheric water vapour, which can drive regional variability in EDW24. More recent work has shown how tropical high-elevation temperatures are modulated by ENSO12.",
